                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Transformations**\n\nOkay, I'm focusing on the grid transformations. I've noted the color changes: blue (1) becomes red (2). Right now, I'm analyzing shape variations. For instance, a 6x3 input yields a 9x3 output. I'm looking at example patterns like [1,0,0], [0,0,1], and [1,0,...] and will determine the output rules.\n\n\n**Calculating Row Additions**\n\nI've been analyzing how many rows get added to the output. It seems the input row number is multiplied by 1.5, then the result is rounded down. For example, an input of 6 rows yields 9 rows (6 * 1.5 = 9), and an input of 5 rows becomes 7 (5 * 1.5 = 7.5, which is rounded down to 7). I now think I have a good handle on how the dimensions change!\n\n\n**Determining Row Generation**\n\nI've got it! The output's height is the input's height plus the floor of the input's height divided by two. I've realized that the extra rows added to the output are simply copies of the input rows, starting from the beginning. Also, all the ones are converted to twos. I'm moving on to testing this pattern.\n\n\n",
